00:44:56.560 --> 00:44:56.760
Yeah.
是啊。

1144
00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:59.660
Lady, 70% accuracy.
天哪，百分之七十的准确率。

1145
00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:02.620
That means 30% of the time he's wrong.
那意味着他有百分之三十的时间是错的。

1146
00:45:02.920 --> 00:45:03.220
Yeah.
是啊。

1147
00:45:03.540 --> 00:45:05.100
He's just made nonsense.
他简直是在胡乱操作。

1148
00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:06.360
That's not good.
那可不好。

1149
00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:07.160
That's not good.
那可不好。

1150
00:45:07.480 --> 00:45:07.820
No.
确实。

1151
00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:10.220
Well, this made me think of a letter that we got.
嗯，这让我想起我们收到的一封信。

1152
00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:11.500
It cracked me up.
它把我逗乐了。

1153
00:45:11.580 --> 00:45:12.860
It is from Claire F.
信是来自澳大利亚布里斯班的克莱尔·F。

1154
00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:14.160
in Brisbane, Australia.
来自澳大利亚布里斯班。

1155
00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:19.880
She wrote in and said, a friend and colleague of mine, let's call her Kate, is a music.
她写信说，我的一位朋友兼同事，我们就叫她凯特吧，是一位音乐老师。

1156
00:45:19.880 --> 00:45:22.340
Oh, I love any letter that's like, let's call her.
哦，我喜欢任何像“我们就叫她……”这样的信。

1157
00:45:22.580 --> 00:45:23.440
Go on.
继续。

1158
00:45:23.780 --> 00:45:30.300
Let's call her Kate, is a music teacher, specifically band and woodwind instruments.
我们就叫她凯特吧，她是一位音乐老师，专门教管乐队和木管乐器。

1159
00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:30.780
Okay.
好的。

1160
00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:36.200
When our department got a new manager, she put in parentheses, let's call him Ron.
当我们的部门来了一位新经理时，她加了个括号，我们就叫他罗恩吧。

1161
00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:37.180
Okay.
好的。

1162
00:45:37.180 --> 00:45:38.140
I love it.
我喜欢这个。

1163
00:45:38.260 --> 00:45:39.740
I love a code name, Claire.
我喜欢代号，克莱尔。

1164
00:45:40.280 --> 00:45:44.900
She said, we all figured out that Ron really didn't know what he was doing quite quickly.
她说，我们很快就发现罗恩根本不知道自己在做什么。

1165
00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:50.400
One day, he called Kate into his office and said that he had been impressed by her work.
有一天，他把凯特叫到办公室，说他对她的工作印象深刻。

1166
00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:56.940
And seeing as she is young and still relatively a new teacher, would she like to have him, Ron,
鉴于她还年轻，而且相对来说是一位新老师，他问她是否愿意让他，也就是罗恩，

1167
00:45:57.160 --> 00:45:57.820
as her mentor?
担任她的导师？

1168
00:45:58.760 --> 00:46:02.420
Kate was hesitant, but what could she say to her boss?
凯特很犹豫，但她能对老板说什么呢？

1169
00:46:02.420 --> 00:46:03.640
So she agreed.
所以她同意了。

1170
00:46:03.800 --> 00:46:09.160
And so began a series of patronizing and unhelpful meetings and rehearsal observations.
于是，一系列居高临下且毫无帮助的会议和排练观察开始了。

1171
00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:17.640
The mentorship came to us swift in when Ron offered Kate a lesson on oboes, an instrument he does not play.
当罗恩主动提出要给凯特上一堂双簧管课时，导师指导就迅速开始了，但他根本不会吹双簧管。

1172
00:46:19.840 --> 00:46:26.300
Claire shares with us that oboes are notoriously hard to play due to the type of reed they use.
克莱尔告诉我们，双簧管因为使用的簧片类型，出了名的难吹。

1173
00:46:26.300 --> 00:46:30.580
There is an incredible amount of back pressure when you blow into it.
当你吹气时，会有巨大的背压。

1174
00:46:31.240 --> 00:46:33.040
Ron started the oboe lesson with Kate.
罗恩开始给凯特上双簧管课。

1175
00:46:33.320 --> 00:46:37.480
He blew into the oboe, made a quick sound, then stopped abruptly with a grunt.
他对着双簧管吹了一下，发出了一声短促的响声，然后突然停下来，哼了一声。

1176
00:46:37.640 --> 00:46:40.300
He left the room in a hurry and did not return.
他匆忙离开了房间，再也没有回来。

1177
00:46:40.300 --> 00:46:42.180
Had he pooped himself?
他是拉裤子里了吗？

1178
00:46:42.580 --> 00:46:43.540
What happened?
发生了什么事？

1179
00:46:43.900 --> 00:46:51.600
It turns out that when Ron blew into the oboe, the back pressure had been so much that he'd given himself a hernia.
原来，当罗恩吹双簧管时，背压太大，导致他自己得了疝气。

1180
00:46:51.820 --> 00:46:52.100
Oh!
哦！

1181
00:46:52.540 --> 00:46:53.060
Oh!
哦！

1182
00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:56.740
And then Claire says that was the end of that.
然后克莱尔说，那件事就那样结束了。

1183
00:46:57.420 --> 00:46:58.560
Oh, my goodness.
天哪。

1184
00:47:00.500 --> 00:47:06.520
So, I just had to share that because I was like, this is the example of not being a great mentor.
所以，我一定要分享这个故事，因为我觉得，这就是一个不称职导师的典型例子。

1185
00:47:06.700 --> 00:47:07.040
Yes.
没错。

1186
00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:08.180
For sure.
确实。

1187
00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:10.220
Thank you, Claire, for writing in.
谢谢你，克莱尔，感谢你的来信。

1188
00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:10.580
Ah!
啊！

1189
00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:14.220
Kelly is now going to burst into the kitchen crying.
凯莉现在要冲进厨房大哭了。

1190
00:47:14.500 --> 00:47:16.720
She said her sister was named prom queen.
她说她姐姐被评为舞会皇后。

1191
00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:18.640
This has obviously really upset her.
这显然让她非常难过。

1192
00:47:18.900 --> 00:47:19.220
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1193
00:47:19.880 --> 00:47:21.500
Erin looks to Kevin like, what do I do?
艾琳看着凯文，好像在问，我该怎么办？

1194
00:47:21.660 --> 00:47:22.660
Kevin says, go to her.
凯文说，去找她。

1195
00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:23.640
Go comfort her.
去安慰她。

1196
00:47:24.100 --> 00:47:26.120
And she gets up and they embrace.
然后她站起来，她们拥抱在一起。

1197
00:47:26.280 --> 00:47:30.180
And Kevin has a talking head where he says, she's going to blossom under his tutelage.
凯文对着镜头说，在她的指导下，她会成长起来的。

1198
00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:38.080
Yeah, and then Oscar has a snarky talking head where he just says, yeah, Kevin can be a great teacher if you want to learn how to eat pizza.
是啊，然后奥斯卡对着镜头尖刻地说，是啊，如果你想学怎么吃披萨，凯文确实能成为一位伟大的老师。

1199
00:47:38.800 --> 00:47:39.100
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1200
00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:40.280
Geez, Oscar.
天哪，奥斯卡。

1201
00:47:40.720 --> 00:47:42.660
Why'd you even have to chime in, bud?
你干嘛非要插嘴呢，伙计？

1202
00:47:43.020 --> 00:47:44.380
Just to be a snark.
就是想刻薄一下。

1203
00:47:44.620 --> 00:47:46.040
Just to be a snarky snark.
就是想刻薄个够。

1204
00:47:46.420 --> 00:47:49.700
Erin is going to end this webisode series with a talking head.
艾琳打算用一段采访镜头来结束这个网络剧系列。

1205
00:47:49.940 --> 00:47:52.600
I want you to know, fakey bangs are back.
我想让你知道，假刘海又流行回来了。

1206
00:47:52.740 --> 00:47:53.000
Okay.
好吧。

1207
00:47:53.000 --> 00:47:54.480
Two minutes, 53 seconds.
两分五十三秒。

1208
00:47:54.660 --> 00:47:55.000
All right.
行吧。

1209
00:47:55.340 --> 00:47:57.200
She says, you can have it all.
她说，你可以拥有一切。

1210
00:47:57.340 --> 00:47:59.540
Be BFFs with Kelly and crunch numbers.
和凯莉成为闺蜜，然后去算账。

1211
00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:01.280
She's one lucky duck.
她真是个幸运儿。

1212
00:48:01.860 --> 00:48:02.280
Okay.
好吧。

1213
00:48:02.520 --> 00:48:03.040
All right.
行吧。

1214
00:48:03.300 --> 00:48:10.880
So she got her friendship back with Kelly because she soothed Kelly through this drama with her sister becoming prom queen.
所以她和凯莉重归于好了，因为她安抚了凯莉，陪她度过了她妹妹当选舞会皇后引发的风波。

1215
00:48:11.060 --> 00:48:13.960
And she gets to be an accountant because now Kevin is her mentor.
而且她还能当会计，因为现在凯文是她的导师。

1216
00:48:13.960 --> 00:48:16.900
So I guess that's her win, win, win.
所以我猜这就是她的三赢局面。

1217
00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:18.120
Oh, wait.
噢，等等。

1218
00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:19.400
Ellie just texted me back.
艾莉刚给我回短信了。

1219
00:48:19.540 --> 00:48:20.480
What did she say?
她说什么了？

1220
00:48:20.500 --> 00:48:20.800
Okay.
好吧。

1221
00:48:20.800 --> 00:48:21.540
Is it an answer?
是答案吗？

1222
00:48:21.720 --> 00:48:25.580
She put in all caps, look, absolute wig, right?
她用了全大写字母，写着：看，绝对是假发，对吧？

1223
00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:26.060
What?
什么？

1224
00:48:26.060 --> 00:48:27.960
And then she has like four question marks.
然后她还加了四个问号。

1225
00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:32.920
And then she says, I have no memory of a wig one way or the other, but those bangs.
接着她说，我不记得有没有戴过假发，但那刘海……

1226
00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:34.760
I mean, what was going on?
我是说，当时到底怎么回事？

1227
00:48:34.840 --> 00:48:36.260
And why would I need a wig?
我为什么需要戴假发？

1228
00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:37.340
It's so confusing.
太让人困惑了。

1229
00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:39.380
Didn't I have bangs already?
我当时不是已经有刘海了吗？

1230
00:48:39.700 --> 00:48:41.580
Was the wig for the French braid?
那假发是为了编法式辫子吗？

1231
00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:43.700
Literally, what is the situation?
到底是什么情况啊？

1232
00:48:44.640 --> 00:48:48.740
So she has no memory, but is also confirming that it is a wig.
所以她没印象，但又确认那是假发。

1233
00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:49.120
Oh, yeah.
噢，是啊。

1234
00:48:49.120 --> 00:48:52.140
She said it's absolutely a wig, but I can't remember why.
她说那绝对是假发，但她想不起来为什么戴。

1235
00:48:52.640 --> 00:48:53.540
Well, there you go.
好吧，这不就有了吗。

1236
00:48:54.720 --> 00:48:55.640
Mystery solved?
谜题解开了？

1237
00:48:56.060 --> 00:48:56.680
Ish.
算是吧。

1238
00:48:56.980 --> 00:48:58.160
We may never know.
我们可能永远都不会知道了。

1239
00:48:58.360 --> 00:48:59.400
We may never know.
我们可能永远都不会知道了。

1240
00:48:59.540 --> 00:49:00.540
That's kind of our thing.
这算是我们的风格吧。

1241
00:49:01.020 --> 00:49:07.040
Well, this whole Mentor Webisode did remind me of a letter that we got from Katie M.
嗯，整个导师网络剧系列让我想起了凯蒂·M寄来的一封信，

1242
00:49:07.140 --> 00:49:07.500
in Richmond,
她来自弗吉尼亚州里士满，

1243
00:49:07.660 --> 00:49:13.640
Virginia that I wanted to read because it's about women helping women in business.
我想读一下，因为信里提到了女性在职场中互助的话题。

1244
00:49:13.880 --> 00:49:14.040
Okay.
好的。

1245
00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:15.220
Because that's what this is.
因为这正是我们现在所做的。

1246
00:49:15.320 --> 00:49:15.460
Yeah.
没错。

1247
00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:17.260
Angela's helping another woman in business.
安吉拉正在帮助另一位职场女性。

1248
00:49:17.640 --> 00:49:22.060
Katie said, I've been working at my company for five years and roughly two years in,
凯蒂说，我在现在的公司工作五年了，大约在两年前，

1249
00:49:22.160 --> 00:49:25.920
I started working with a young woman who was about five years older than me.
我开始和一位比我大五岁左右的年轻女性共事。

1250
00:49:26.060 --> 00:49:28.440
And she very quickly became my work BFF.
她很快就成了我的职场闺蜜。

1251
00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:33.580
We tackled many projects together and also made it through some rough times from sexist
我们一起共事过许多项目，也共同经历过一些艰难时刻，从性别歧视的

1252
00:49:33.580 --> 00:49:36.160
co-workers to surviving a series of layoffs.
同事到熬过一系列裁员。

1253
00:49:36.960 --> 00:49:40.120
After a big restructuring, she ended up being on another team.
在一次大规模重组后，她被调到了另一个团队。

1254
00:49:40.120 --> 00:49:44.340
And while we knew we wouldn't be able to work together as much, we still made an effort to
虽然我们知道以后不能像以前那样经常共事了，但我们还是努力

1255
00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:46.280
have a long chat at least once a month.
保持每个月至少长谈一次。

1256
00:49:46.660 --> 00:49:51.620
It was through these chats that we began talking seriously about career trajectories, and she
正是通过这些谈话，我们开始认真探讨职业发展轨迹，她

1257
00:49:51.620 --> 00:49:56.680
inspired me into taking on more serious projects on my team and advocating for myself.
激励我在团队中承担更重要的项目，并学会为自己争取权益。

1258
00:49:57.180 --> 00:50:02.020
At the end of last year, a position opened up on her new team, and she went above and beyond in
去年年底，她所在的团队出现了一个职位空缺，她不遗余力地

1259
00:50:02.020 --> 00:50:05.760
helping me prepare for interviews, market myself, and boost my confidence.
帮我准备面试、推销自己，并增强我的自信心。

1260
00:50:06.320 --> 00:50:09.340
Because of her, I ended up getting a serious promotion.
多亏了她，我最终获得了一次重要的晋升。

1261
00:50:09.760 --> 00:50:12.800
And I am now directly in the field I want to be.
现在我终于进入了自己心仪的领域。

1262
00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:18.960
Because of her, for the first time in my professional career, I am on an incredible team of all women.
多亏了她，我的职业生涯中第一次加入了一个全由女性组成的优秀团队。

1263
00:50:19.660 --> 00:50:26.660
Let's break the narrative of women kicking the ladder down and promote stories like mine of women lifting each other up.
让我们打破“女性拆台”的刻板印象，多宣传像我这样女性互相扶持的故事吧。

1264
00:50:26.800 --> 00:50:28.020
Aw, that's great.
噢，太棒了。

1265
00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:29.560
I loved that.
我喜欢这个故事。

1266
00:50:29.660 --> 00:50:30.480
Thank you, Katie.
谢谢你，凯蒂。

1267
00:50:31.080 --> 00:50:33.940
And you know who it made me think of was Terri Weinberg.
你知道吗，这让我想起了泰瑞·温伯格。

1268
00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:35.160
Oh, yes.
噢，是的。

1269
00:50:35.380 --> 00:50:38.300
Terri Weinberg was one of the executive producers on The Office.
泰瑞·温伯格是《办公室》的执行制片人之一。

1270
00:50:38.600 --> 00:50:43.720
She worked for Reveille, which was the company that owned the rights to the British office.
她当时效力于Reveille公司，该公司拥有英版《办公室》的版权。

1271
00:50:43.720 --> 00:50:49.580
And they were part of developing the show for an American audience from the very beginning.
从一开始，他们就参与了美版剧集的开发工作。

1272
00:50:49.780 --> 00:50:51.120
Terri was part of our auditions.
泰瑞参与了我们的试镜。

1273
00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:52.700
She was on the set of the pilot.
她就在试播集的片场。

1274
00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:55.640
She is just an amazing woman.
她真是一位了不起的女性。

1275
00:50:55.900 --> 00:51:02.460
And after The Office was over, and I wanted to maybe get into some producing myself,
《办公室》结束后，我想尝试自己做制片人，

1276
00:51:03.180 --> 00:51:06.580
I had gotten a script, and I didn't know where to start.
我当时拿到一个剧本，却不知道从何下手。

1277
00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:07.780
I really liked it.
我真的很喜欢它。

1278
00:51:07.940 --> 00:51:09.620
It was from a writer submitted it to me.
是一位编剧投给我的。

1279
00:51:09.620 --> 00:51:12.860
And I thought, how do I get this into the hands of the studios?
我在想，我该怎么把它送到制片厂手里呢？

1280
00:51:13.060 --> 00:51:14.740
Like, how do I try to sell this?
比如，我该怎么尝试把它卖出去？

1281
00:51:14.860 --> 00:51:15.020
Right, right.
对，没错。

1282
00:51:15.620 --> 00:51:17.180
And I called Terri.
于是我给泰瑞打了电话。

1283
00:51:18.200 --> 00:51:19.980
And Terri said, send it to me.
泰瑞说，发给我吧。

1284
00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:20.800
I'll read it.
我会看的。

1285
00:51:20.900 --> 00:51:21.600
Let's have lunch.
我们一起吃个午饭吧。

1286
00:51:22.040 --> 00:51:24.240
I mean, do you know how much—she's so busy.
我的意思是，你知道她有多忙吗？

1287
00:51:24.240 --> 00:51:28.860
And like, she took the time to read this thing that I thought maybe I could do something with.
她竟然抽出时间来读这个我以为或许能做点什么的东西。

1288
00:51:29.120 --> 00:51:30.220
She had lunch with me.
她和我一起吃了午饭。

1289
00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:31.440
She gave me all her thoughts.
她给了我她所有的想法。

1290
00:51:31.800 --> 00:51:34.080
She laid out, like, here's what I would do.
她给我列出了建议，比如，我会怎么做。

1291
00:51:34.100 --> 00:51:35.160
Here's what I wouldn't do.
我不会怎么做。

1292
00:51:35.480 --> 00:51:37.480
And I ended up selling it to Warner Brothers.
最后我把它卖给了华纳兄弟。

1293
00:51:37.480 --> 00:51:43.440
Now, the show didn't get made, but under Terri's guidance, like—and she said,
虽然那部剧后来没拍成，但在泰瑞的指导下——她说，

1294
00:51:43.760 --> 00:51:45.660
always call me about stuff like this, Jenna.
珍娜，以后有这种事随时打给我。

1295
00:51:45.900 --> 00:51:46.220
Always.
随时。

1296
00:51:46.580 --> 00:51:46.760
Yeah.
是啊。

1297
00:51:46.980 --> 00:51:48.200
This is what I do.
这就是我的工作。

1298
00:51:48.380 --> 00:51:49.960
Like, this is what I love doing.
这就是我热爱的事业。

1299
00:51:50.160 --> 00:51:54.180
I love helping other women in this industry make their mark.
我热爱帮助这个行业里的其他女性留下她们的印记。

1300
00:51:54.460 --> 00:51:54.620
Yeah.
是啊。

1301
00:51:55.420 --> 00:52:01.140
Yeah, she has been incredibly supportive to us over all these years, is still in touch with us,
没错，这些年来她一直非常支持我们，至今仍与我们保持联系，

1302
00:52:01.220 --> 00:52:03.060
is still such a huge champion.
依然是我们坚定的支持者。

1303
00:52:03.460 --> 00:52:07.100
Whatever marker happens in our career, she celebrates it.
无论我们的职业生涯有什么里程碑，她都会为我们庆祝。

1304
00:52:07.100 --> 00:52:10.180
When we wrote our book, when we got the podcast, just on and on.
我们写书时、做播客时，诸如此类，不胜枚举。

1305
00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:15.780
I'm sure if you look in our comments on Instagram, you're going to see cheerleading from Terri
我相信如果你去翻我们的 Instagram 评论区，一定会看到 Terri

1306
00:52:15.780 --> 00:52:16.820
in our posts.
在我们帖子下的加油打气。

1307
00:52:17.060 --> 00:52:17.300
Yeah.
是的。

1308
00:52:17.640 --> 00:52:18.500
She's the beSt.
她真是太棒了。

1309
00:52:18.620 --> 00:52:18.780
Yeah.
没错。

1310
00:52:19.540 --> 00:52:22.740
Well, listen, that ends the webisode series.
好了，听着，网络系列节目到这里就结束了。

1311
00:52:23.060 --> 00:52:23.400
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

1312
00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:25.480
It was such a pleasure to watch.
观看这个系列真是种享受。

1313
00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:25.940
It was.
确实。

1314
00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:26.760
It was really fun.
真的很有趣。

1315
00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:31.360
But before we go, we wanted to share a few more of your stories of mentorship and our own.
但在结束之前，我们想再分享几个你们关于导师的故事，以及我们自己的故事。

1316
00:52:31.360 --> 00:52:34.960
I'm going to kick us off with a letter that I just loved.
我先来读一封我特别喜欢的信。

1317
00:52:35.180 --> 00:52:37.740
It's from Kristen from Virginia.
这封信来自弗吉尼亚州的 Kristen。

1318
00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:39.540
And Kristen says,
Kristen 说，

1319
00:52:39.740 --> 00:52:44.500
When I was in my mid-twenties, I became infatuated with calligraphy.
我二十多岁时迷上了书法。

1320
00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:49.960
I received a gift certificate to take classes with a local woman named Betsy.
我收到了一张礼券，可以去上一位当地女士 Betsy 的课。

1321
00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:56.180
She was probably in her 70s and was left-handed, which is a challenge for calligraphers.
她当时大概 70 多岁，是左撇子，这对书法家来说是个挑战。

1322
00:52:56.560 --> 00:52:58.340
So she wrote upside down.
所以她是倒着写的。

1323
00:52:58.500 --> 00:53:00.400
It was incredible to watch.
看她写字真是太神奇了。

1324
00:53:00.600 --> 00:53:00.900
Wow.
哇。

1325
00:53:01.180 --> 00:53:01.360
Yeah.
是啊。

1326
00:53:01.740 --> 00:53:06.600
She was so thrilled someone under the age of 50 actually wanted to learn calligraphy.
她非常激动，因为终于有个 50 岁以下的人想学书法了。

1327
00:53:06.760 --> 00:53:09.760
I was hooked on the art for my very first class with her.
从第一节课开始，我就彻底迷上了这门艺术。

1328
00:53:09.920 --> 00:53:13.980
I signed up for every class she offered and eventually asked her to be my mentor.
我报名了她开设的所有课程，最终还请她担任我的导师。

1329
00:53:13.980 --> 00:53:19.300
I started taking private lessons, and she exposed me to so much about the art,
我开始上私教课，她让我接触到了这门艺术的方方面面，

1330
00:53:19.560 --> 00:53:25.560
different tools and supplies, lettering styles, artists, historical references, and so much more.
不同的工具和用品、字体风格、艺术家、历史参考资料等等，不胜枚举。

1331
00:53:25.780 --> 00:53:29.480
I learned something new every week, and it really put me ahead
我每周都能学到新东西，这真的让我在后来

1332
00:53:29.480 --> 00:53:32.600
when I started engaging in professional work and networking.
开始从事专业工作和建立人脉时领先了一步。

1333
00:53:33.020 --> 00:53:36.820
Because of her, I started a business, wrote a book, started teaching,
因为她，我创办了公司，写了书，开始教学，

1334
00:53:37.020 --> 00:53:40.540
and became very involved with a professional calligraphy organization,
并积极参与了一个专业的书法组织，

1335
00:53:40.540 --> 00:53:42.980
of which I am currently the president.
我现在还是该组织的会长。

1336
00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:43.840
Wow.
哇。

1337
00:53:43.980 --> 00:53:44.680
I know.
我知道。

1338
00:53:44.980 --> 00:53:50.380
In 2020, Betsy was diagnosed with advanced ALS and was unable to continue working.
2020 年，Betsy 被诊断出患有晚期渐冻症（ALS），无法继续工作。

1339
00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:55.500
Shortly before she passed away, she referred a few jobs to me and told me that I was one of her
在她去世前不久，她给我介绍了几份工作，并告诉我我是她

1340
00:53:55.500 --> 00:53:56.300
best students.
最好的学生之一。

1341
00:53:56.720 --> 00:53:58.920
And Kristen put in parentheses, crying.
Kristen 在括号里写道：正在哭泣。

1342
00:53:59.480 --> 00:54:00.700
I am crying.
我也在哭。

1343
00:54:00.880 --> 00:54:01.060
Yeah.
是啊。

1344
00:54:01.320 --> 00:54:01.660
Chills.
起鸡皮疙瘩了。

1345
00:54:02.120 --> 00:54:05.880
She goes on to say she wished me the best of luck with the calligraphy community.
她接着说，祝我在书法界一切顺利。

1346
00:54:06.420 --> 00:54:10.460
This year, I got to host a calligraphy conference in the very city she taught me in,
今年，我有幸在她当年教我的城市举办了一场书法大会，

1347
00:54:10.560 --> 00:54:12.840
and it's a really beautiful, full-circle moment for me.
对我来说，这真是一个圆满而美好的时刻。

1348
00:54:12.840 --> 00:54:15.400
I wouldn't be where I am today without Betsy.
没有 Betsy，就不会有今天的我。

1349
00:54:15.640 --> 00:54:18.540
I think of Betsy often and how selfless she was.
我经常想起 Betsy，想起她有多么无私。

1350
00:54:18.720 --> 00:54:20.000
She had no secrets.
她毫无保留。

1351
00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:22.400
She just wanted to pass along her knowledge.
她只是想把自己的知识传授下去。

1352
00:54:22.700 --> 00:54:27.560
The style she taught me, called Copper Plate, is actually a style she didn't love love,
她教我的那种风格叫铜版体，其实她本人并不是特别喜欢，

1353
00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:30.980
but she taught it in memory of a dear friend of hers that passed away.
但她教这个是为了纪念她一位已故的挚友。

1354
00:54:31.500 --> 00:54:35.820
The mindset of generosity and legacy is something I carry with me daily.
那种慷慨和传承的精神，我每天都铭记在心。

1355
00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:37.400
That's making me tear up.
这让我都要掉眼泪了。

1356
00:54:37.780 --> 00:54:38.740
Yeah, me too.
是啊，我也是。

1357
00:54:39.200 --> 00:54:44.180
Betsy was and continues to be an inspiration to me, and I'm so lucky I had the chance to
贝齐过去是，现在依然是我灵感的源泉，我真的很幸运能有机会

1358
00:54:44.180 --> 00:54:44.800
learn from her.
向她学习。

1359
00:54:45.100 --> 00:54:49.680
Thanks for the opportunity to share about this mentorship experience that means so much
感谢你们给我这个机会，让我分享这段对我意义重大的

1360
00:54:49.680 --> 00:54:50.040
to me.
导师经历。

1361
00:54:51.360 --> 00:54:57.960
I just love that legacy that Betsy has that she passed down.
我真的很喜欢贝齐留下的那种传承，她把它传给了后人。

1362
00:54:58.380 --> 00:54:59.900
Yeah, thank you.
是的，谢谢你。

1363
00:54:59.920 --> 00:55:02.900
She changed that woman's life, truly.
她真的改变了那个女人的生活。

1364
00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:03.540
Truly.
确实如此。

1365
00:55:04.020 --> 00:55:06.740
It's become her whole career now.
这现在已经成了她毕生的事业。

1366
00:55:06.980 --> 00:55:12.420
It just took such time to share her knowledge and didn't hold any of it back, gave it all
她花了很多时间去分享自己的知识，毫无保留，倾囊相授，

1367
00:55:12.420 --> 00:55:15.560
freely to her, to share with her, to build her up.
为了分享，为了成就她。

1368
00:55:15.560 --> 00:55:17.600
Kristen, thank you so much for sharing.
克里斯汀，非常感谢你的分享。

1369
00:55:17.760 --> 00:55:21.640
That letter really touched us, and we're so happy now to know about Betsy.
那封信真的触动了我们，我们现在很高兴能认识贝齐。

1370
00:55:21.880 --> 00:55:22.040
Yeah.
是的。

1371
00:55:22.660 --> 00:55:26.060
Angela, do you want to share about a mentor you've had in your life?
安吉拉，你想分享一下你生命中的导师吗？

1372
00:55:26.720 --> 00:55:27.160
Yes.
好的。

1373
00:55:27.360 --> 00:55:33.900
We both decided we wanted to share for this episode about someone who meant a lot to us
我们俩都决定在这一期节目里分享一位对我们意义重大、

1374
00:55:33.900 --> 00:55:34.840
and mentored us.
指导过我们的人。

1375
00:55:35.120 --> 00:55:42.120
And, you know, I just knew immediately who I was going to share about, and it's my grandmother,
你知道，我立刻就知道我要分享谁了，那就是我的祖母，

1376
00:55:42.320 --> 00:55:43.100
Lena May Kinsey.
莉娜·梅·金西。

1377
00:55:44.340 --> 00:55:50.740
So much of who I am, of my values, of the things that I connect with that mean something
我之所以成为现在的我，我的价值观，以及我所珍视的那些事物，

1378
00:55:50.740 --> 00:55:52.920
to me, they all started with her.
这一切都源于她。

1379
00:55:53.500 --> 00:55:54.660
I've shared this before.
我以前也分享过。

1380
00:55:54.660 --> 00:56:00.400
I moved around a lot as a kid, just with my dad's job, but my grandmother's home was
小时候因为父亲工作的关系，我经常搬家，但祖母的家

1381
00:56:00.400 --> 00:56:02.580
the one constant place I had.
是我唯一不变的港湾。

1382
00:56:02.720 --> 00:56:08.560
And I would go and stay with her in the summers, and looking back on that time, oh my gosh, I
夏天我会去她那里住，回想起那段时光，天哪，我

1383
00:56:08.560 --> 00:56:09.540
just cherish it.
真的非常珍惜。

1384
00:56:09.840 --> 00:56:12.340
She just taught me so many things.
她教会了我很多东西。

1385
00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:15.940
I mean, I started to list them out in no particular order.
我是说，我开始列举它们，不分先后。

1386
00:56:16.460 --> 00:56:19.980
She taught me how to work a riding lawnmower.
她教我怎么开乘坐式割草机。

1387
00:56:21.060 --> 00:56:22.860
She taught me how to drive a car.
她教我怎么开车。

1388
00:56:23.460 --> 00:56:24.940
She taught me how to crochet.
她教我怎么钩针编织。

1389
00:56:26.000 --> 00:56:28.040
She tried to teach me how to quilt.
她试着教我怎么做拼布被。

1390
00:56:28.260 --> 00:56:29.520
I was just not very patient.
我当时没什么耐心。

1391
00:56:29.780 --> 00:56:31.000
She was an amazing quilter.
她是一位了不起的拼布高手。

1392
00:56:31.660 --> 00:56:34.700
She would put us all to work, all of her grandkids in her garden.
她会让我们所有孙辈都在她的花园里干活。

1393
00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:36.920
The woman could grow anything.
那个女人什么都能种出来。

1394
00:56:37.740 --> 00:56:40.720
And I would spend hours with her in her kitchen.
我会在她的厨房里陪她待上好几个小时。

1395
00:56:41.160 --> 00:56:45.800
She always had something from her garden that she was making or our chores that she would
她总是会用花园里的食材做些什么，或者安排我们

1396
00:56:45.800 --> 00:56:46.380
have us do.
做些家务。

1397
00:56:46.540 --> 00:56:50.060
And it's so interesting because, you know, your parents might give you a chore, and you're
很有意思的是，你知道，父母让你做家务时，你可能会

1398
00:56:50.060 --> 00:56:50.520
like, ugh.
觉得很烦。

1399
00:56:50.780 --> 00:56:54.260
But my grandmother would give me a chore, and I delighted in it.
但祖母让我做家务时，我却乐在其中。

1400
00:56:54.260 --> 00:56:55.360
I was like, what can I do?
我当时会问，我能帮什么忙？

1401
00:56:56.060 --> 00:57:00.880
One time, she taught me how to make homemade ice cream using the peaches that she had picked
有一次，她教我怎么用她亲手采摘的桃子制作自制冰淇淋，

1402
00:57:00.880 --> 00:57:02.100
from her garden.
那些桃子就来自她的花园。

1403
00:57:02.320 --> 00:57:08.660
And, you know, in sort of going back and remembering, I have this book that she made me, and I started
你知道，在回首往事时，我翻开了她为我制作的那本书，并开始

1404
00:57:08.660 --> 00:57:09.520
to go through it.
仔细阅读。

1405
00:57:09.720 --> 00:57:12.140
I had saved all of her cards and letters.
我保存了她写给我的所有卡片和信件。

1406
00:57:12.380 --> 00:57:15.840
She was a very big believer in writing letters and cards.
她非常笃信写信和寄卡片的意义。

1407
00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:19.260
You got a card every year on your birthday to all of her grandkids.
她所有的孙辈每年生日都能收到她寄来的卡片。

1408
00:57:19.260 --> 00:57:21.300
She had a ton, and her great-grandchildren.
她有很多孙辈，还有曾孙辈。

1409
00:57:21.300 --> 00:57:22.960
But she also wrote me a lot of letters.
但她也给我写过很多信。

1410
00:57:23.600 --> 00:57:29.900
She put together this whole book for me when I asked her one time, once, I said, I would
有一次我随口提了一句，说我想

1411
00:57:29.900 --> 00:57:31.780
love to know more about our family tree.
多了解一下我们的家族树，她就为我整理了整整一本书。

1412
00:57:32.020 --> 00:57:39.620
She put together a whole book for me of birth certificates and marriage license and just on
她把出生证明、结婚证书以及关于我们家族历史的各种资料，

1413
00:57:39.620 --> 00:57:41.740
and on about our family history.
统统整理成册交给了我。

1414
00:57:42.700 --> 00:57:44.600
Jenna, here's a picture of all her peaches.
珍娜，这是她种的那些桃子的照片。

1415
00:57:44.920 --> 00:57:48.060
She would just send me pictures from her yard.
她经常会给我发她院子里的照片。

1416
00:57:48.060 --> 00:57:56.220
And these are the things that just mean so much to me that I still find ground me in my life now.
这些对我来说意义非凡，至今仍是我生活中的精神支柱。

1417
00:57:56.580 --> 00:57:58.420
She had a real heart for service.
她拥有一颗热衷于服务他人的心。

1418
00:57:58.440 --> 00:58:00.500
She volunteered in her community.
她经常在社区做志愿者。

1419
00:58:01.100 --> 00:58:06.160
She was a firm believer in, if you don't have something nice to say, then don't say anything at all.
她坚信，如果你说不出什么好话，那就什么都别说。

1420
00:58:07.120 --> 00:58:10.880
And I have these tangible mementos of her now.
现在，我拥有这些关于她的珍贵纪念品。

1421
00:58:11.080 --> 00:58:13.320
She made us all quilts by hand.
她亲手为我们每个人缝制了被子。

1422
00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:18.220
She made each of her grandkids a little lacy cover for their baby Bible.
她还为每个孙辈的婴儿圣经缝制了蕾丝封套。

1423
00:58:18.940 --> 00:58:25.780
But I look now in my own home, and I see all of these touchstones that are her.
现在环顾我的家，我能看到所有这些属于她的印记。

1424
00:58:27.000 --> 00:58:30.640
If you went in her house, she had so many pictures.
如果你去过她家，会发现她摆放了特别多的照片。

1425
00:58:30.860 --> 00:58:35.120
Like, it almost looked cluttered because she just wanted everyone to see her family.
看起来甚至有点杂乱，因为她就是想让每个人都能看到她的家人。

1426
00:58:35.580 --> 00:58:37.820
And my house is so full of pictures.
而我的家里也摆满了照片。

1427
00:58:38.800 --> 00:58:43.000
I once had a friend who was dating this new gal, and she was very chic.
有一次，我有个朋友带新女友来家里，那女孩非常时髦。

1428
00:58:43.160 --> 00:58:46.320
And she came over to my house, and she walked in, and she goes,
她走进我家，然后说，

1429
00:58:46.440 --> 00:58:48.960
Oh, she goes, I feel like I'm in a grandma's house.
“噢，”她说，“感觉就像进了奶奶家一样。”

1430
00:58:49.320 --> 00:58:52.000
And I think she meant it to be snarky.
我觉得她这话带点讽刺意味。

1431
00:58:52.220 --> 00:58:53.980
But I took it as such a compliment.
但我却把它当作一种赞美。

1432
00:58:54.420 --> 00:59:00.160
I mean, my kitchen cabinets, I purposely picked the color that was my grandmother's wallpaper in her kitchen.
我是说，我厨房橱柜的颜色，就是特意选的和我祖母厨房壁纸一样的颜色。

1433
00:59:00.900 --> 00:59:03.160
Anyway, she was just full of grace and kindness.
总之，她充满了优雅与善良。

1434
00:59:03.340 --> 00:59:04.700
She was hardworking and earneSt.
她勤劳而真诚。

1435
00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:06.800
I just loved and admired her.
我深爱并敬仰着她。

1436
00:59:07.320 --> 00:59:11.840
And as I went back through her letters and cards that she sent me over the years that I saved,
当我翻看这些年来她寄给我并被我珍藏的信件和卡片时，

1437
00:59:12.440 --> 00:59:16.680
I wanted to share just one sentence that she wrote to me.
我想分享她写给我的一句话。

1438
00:59:17.740 --> 00:59:19.900
And it just kind of sums everything up.
这句话简直概括了一切。

1439
00:59:20.280 --> 00:59:23.240
She had written me a card because I had just moved to Los Angeles.
当时我刚搬到洛杉矶，她给我寄了一张卡片。

1440
00:59:23.620 --> 00:59:28.740
And I had shared with her that I was really struggling on how to start this dream of being an actor.
我曾向她倾诉，说我对于如何开启演员梦想感到非常迷茫。

1441
00:59:28.920 --> 00:59:30.220
No one in my family was an actor.
我们家里没人做过演员。

1442
00:59:30.320 --> 00:59:34.120
This was a crazy thing for me to go to Los Angeles and do this.
去洛杉矶闯荡对我来说是一件疯狂的事。

1443
00:59:34.900 --> 00:59:38.380
And the first sentence of her card she sent me said this,
而她寄给我的那张卡片，第一句话就是这样写的：

1444
00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:40.720
I believe you are on your way.
“我相信你正在通往成功的路上。”

1445
00:59:40.980 --> 00:59:45.760
There may be bumps in the road, but that makes the smooth part seem much better.
“路上或许会有坎坷，但正是因为有了这些，平坦的路才显得更加美好。”

1446
00:59:48.580 --> 00:59:50.540
So, she's one of my biggest mentors.
所以，她是我人生中最重要的导师之一。

1447
00:59:50.780 --> 00:59:55.480
And I, you know, my happy place is my yard with my plants and my hummingbirds.
你知道，我最快乐的地方就是我的院子，那里有我的花草和蜂鸟。

1448
00:59:55.640 --> 00:59:58.240
She had the most amazing hummingbird feeder.
她曾拥有一个最棒的蜂鸟喂食器。

1449
00:59:58.520 --> 01:00:01.640
It was just a regular one, but it hung outside her kitchen window.
那只是个普通的喂食器，但挂在她厨房窗外。

1450
01:00:01.800 --> 01:00:03.340
And she would get a gazillion hummingbirds.
总会有成千上万只蜂鸟飞来。

1451
01:00:03.340 --> 01:00:06.880
And we would just sit there and visit at her kitchen table.
我们就坐在她家的餐桌旁聊天。

1452
01:00:07.280 --> 01:00:13.200
And yeah, I'm so thankful that I had her in my life.
真的，我很庆幸生命中有她的存在。

1453
01:00:13.700 --> 01:00:18.780
Lady, I am so, like, moved by your description of her because
天哪，你对她的描述真的让我很感动，因为

1454
01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:24.240
everything you're saying, it is just living through you.
你所说的一切，仿佛就在你身上重现。

1455
01:00:24.320 --> 01:00:26.320
Like, so clearly to me as your friend.
作为你的朋友，我看得一清二楚。

1456
01:00:26.560 --> 01:00:30.100
Like, when you said that when you walked in her house, she had tons of pictures.
比如你说当你走进她家时，那里摆满了照片。

1457
01:00:30.100 --> 01:00:31.880
I mean, this is a thing about you.
我是说，这和你很像。

1458
01:00:32.280 --> 01:00:34.740
Like, guys, Angela's fridge is covered.
伙计们，安吉拉的冰箱上贴满了照片。

1459
01:00:34.920 --> 01:00:36.560
You put pictures in frames.
你总是把照片装进相框里。

1460
01:00:36.560 --> 01:00:38.960
And I love how your home feels.
我很喜欢你家给人的感觉。

1461
01:00:39.340 --> 01:00:43.600
But also, like, how many pictures of your hydrangea can you take?
但话说回来，你的绣球花到底能拍多少张照片啊？

1462
01:00:43.600 --> 01:00:44.140
I know.
我知道。

1463
01:00:44.240 --> 01:00:46.060
And that's the picture of the peaches right there.
还有那张桃子的照片就在那儿。

1464
01:00:46.280 --> 01:00:46.460
I know.
我知道。

1465
01:00:46.620 --> 01:00:47.060
You know?
你懂吗？

1466
01:00:47.340 --> 01:00:50.820
Like, she is living through you.
她仿佛就在你身上活着。

1467
01:00:51.020 --> 01:00:55.260
Like, this, I see her impact on you.
我能清楚地看到她对你的影响。

1468
01:00:55.260 --> 01:00:59.520
So clearly, as you described this, all the handwritten notes you've sent to me,
当你描述这一切时，我看得非常清楚，你发给我的所有手写便条，

1469
01:00:59.860 --> 01:01:05.160
all of your digital clutter, it's all, like, I'm just seeing her.
你所有的数字杂物，这一切，我仿佛都看到了她。

1470
01:01:05.460 --> 01:01:05.720
Yeah.
是的。

1471
01:01:06.400 --> 01:01:08.620
Oh, thank you for sharing that.
噢，谢谢你的分享。

1472
01:01:08.840 --> 01:01:09.200
Yeah.
是的。

1473
01:01:09.420 --> 01:01:13.340
I feel like even after all these years, I just got to know you even more deeply
我觉得即使过了这么多年，因为你的分享，

1474
01:01:14.020 --> 01:01:15.300
because you shared that.
我反而更深入地了解你了。

1475
01:01:15.520 --> 01:01:17.760
Oh, you would have just loved her.
噢，你一定会很喜欢她的。

1476
01:01:17.900 --> 01:01:18.760
I have no doubt.
我对此深信不疑。

1477
01:01:18.760 --> 01:01:19.540
I love you.
我爱你。

1478
01:01:20.180 --> 01:01:22.960
Well, Jenna, I know you have a letter that really moved you
那么，珍娜，我知道你有一封让你深受感动的信，

1479
01:01:22.960 --> 01:01:25.340
that was sent in to us that you would like to share.
那是寄给我们的，你想和大家分享一下。

1480
01:01:25.600 --> 01:01:26.540
I do.
是的，我想分享。

1481
01:01:26.920 --> 01:01:29.460
So this letter was from Omar R.
这封信来自布鲁克林威廉斯堡的奥马尔·R。

1482
01:01:29.780 --> 01:01:31.040
in Williamsburg, Brooklyn.
这是奥马尔写的内容。

1483
01:01:31.660 --> 01:01:33.100
Here's what Omar said.
首先，我是从第一天就开始听节目的听众。

1484
01:01:33.600 --> 01:01:35.860
First of all, day one listener here.
你们的节目和播客给我带来了很多快乐。

1485
01:01:36.240 --> 01:01:38.960
Your show and podcast has brought me so much joy.
每当我感到焦虑时，听你们女士们聊天真的能让我放松下来，

1486
01:01:39.120 --> 01:01:43.000
And anytime I have anxiety, hearing you ladies talk really relaxes me
让我觉得一切都会好起来的。

1487
01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:45.360
and makes me feel like everything will be okay.
我的导师故事很独特，因为我年轻时并不知道他们是我的导师。

1488
01:01:46.180 --> 01:01:51.140
My mentor story is unique in that I didn't know they were my mentors when I was younger.
我21岁时在布鲁克林的一家酒吧，两位年长的绅士坐在我旁边，

1489
01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:57.080
I was 21 years old at a bar in Brooklyn and two older gentlemen sat next to me
他们无意中听到我和朋友聊天，就跟我打了招呼。

1490
01:01:57.080 --> 01:02:00.240
and they overheard me talking to my friends and they said hi.
他们刚从法国搬到美国。

1491
01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:03.940
They had just moved to the United States from France.
我们聊了几乎一整晚，发现他们养了一只狗，

1492
01:02:04.660 --> 01:02:08.700
We ended up talking most of the night and found out they had a dog who needed walking
每隔一个月他们回法国出差时，就需要有人帮忙遛狗和照顾它。

1493
01:02:08.700 --> 01:02:12.580
and taking care of every other month when they were back in France for business.
他们教会了我所知道的一切礼仪。

1494
01:02:13.340 --> 01:02:16.140
They taught me everything I know about etiquette.
在我21岁时，他们带我参加了人生中第一次晚宴、活动和百老汇演出。

1495
01:02:16.140 --> 01:02:21.780
They brought me to my first ever dinner parties, events, Broadway shows at 21.
我当时不懂礼节，老实说，我也不太懂什么是尊重。

1496
01:02:22.160 --> 01:02:26.460
I didn't know about decorum and honestly, I didn't know very much about respect.
他们相信我，总是鼓励我努力工作，成就一番事业。

1497
01:02:27.160 --> 01:02:31.740
They believed in me and always encouraged me to work hard and make something with myself.
他们的善良和激励正是我这个迷茫的21岁青年所需要的。

1498
01:02:32.300 --> 01:02:36.480
Their kindness and motivation was what I needed as a lost 21-year-old.
我帮他们遛狗，帮他们看家，持续了好几年。

1499
01:02:36.800 --> 01:02:39.900
I walked their dog and sat there home for years.
几年前他们搬回了法国，我一直没能好好地感谢他们。

1500
01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:44.580
They moved back to France a few years ago and I never fully got to thank them
我一直没能好好地感谢他们。

1501
01:02:44.580 --> 01:02:46.620
for everything they taught me about life.
感谢他们教会我的一切人生道理。

1502
01:02:47.360 --> 01:02:50.460
So, say yes to those who are older and wiser.
所以，要对那些年长且睿智的人说“好”。

1503
01:02:50.820 --> 01:02:53.820
Say yes to those who motivate you to pursue your dreaMs.
要对那些激励你追求梦想的人说“好”。

1504
01:02:54.120 --> 01:02:57.620
Say yes to those who have positive intent for your life.
要对那些对你的人生怀有善意的人说“好”。

1505
01:02:58.320 --> 01:03:01.800
I now help run a creative department for a tech company,
我现在在一家科技公司负责一个创意部门，

1506
01:03:02.000 --> 01:03:05.780
a position I would have never had if they didn't always believe in me
如果不是他们始终相信我，

1507
01:03:05.780 --> 01:03:08.080
to pursue my dreams and never give up.
鼓励我追求梦想、永不放弃，我绝不可能得到这个职位。

1508
01:03:08.080 --> 01:03:10.040
And then Omar said,
然后奥马尔说，

1509
01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:11.780
Thank you, ladies, for all that you do.
“谢谢你们，女士们，感谢你们所做的一切。”

1510
01:03:11.940 --> 01:03:15.020
Looking forward to hearing more and more stories y'all have to share.
期待听到你们分享更多更多的故事。

1511
01:03:15.500 --> 01:03:18.700
Also, finally watched The Edge because of your podcaSt.
另外，因为你们的播客，我终于看了《边缘》。

1512
01:03:18.700 --> 01:03:19.540
Oh my gosh.
天哪。

1513
01:03:19.800 --> 01:03:21.640
And I now say to my friends,
我现在也常对朋友们说，

1514
01:03:21.780 --> 01:03:24.600
a good plan today is better than a perfect plan tomorrow.
“今天的一个好计划，胜过明天的完美计划。”

1515
01:03:25.520 --> 01:03:28.680
And I have now become a mentor to my younger friends.
我现在也成了年轻朋友们的导师。

1516
01:03:28.840 --> 01:03:34.000
And I owe it all to two random Frenchmen I met in a bar when I was 21.
这一切都要归功于我21岁时在酒吧遇到的两个素不相识的法国人。

1517
01:03:34.540 --> 01:03:35.880
I'm 31 now.
我现在31岁了。

1518
01:03:36.780 --> 01:03:37.200
Aww.
噢。

1519
01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:38.800
I just loved it.
我太喜欢这个故事了。

1520
01:03:39.060 --> 01:03:43.320
I did too because it's the kindness and, you know, they were strangers.
我也很喜欢，因为这体现了善意，而且，你知道，他们当时只是陌生人。

1521
01:03:43.840 --> 01:03:45.240
They were kind.
他们很善良。

1522
01:03:45.680 --> 01:03:52.920
And they helped this young person, like, have a sense of security, community.
他们帮助了一个年轻人，让她感受到了一种安全感和归属感。

1523
01:03:54.040 --> 01:03:56.420
And that's a lifelong friendship.
那是一段终身的友谊。

1524
01:03:56.740 --> 01:03:56.920
Yeah.
没错。

1525
01:03:57.220 --> 01:03:57.920
That's wonderful.
那太棒了。

1526
01:03:58.280 --> 01:03:58.620
Yeah.
是啊。

1527
01:03:58.920 --> 01:04:02.760
Well, Jenna, I know you have a really special mentor you would like to share about.
那么，珍娜，我知道你有一位非常特别的导师想要分享。

1528
01:04:02.760 --> 01:04:03.980
I would.
是的，我想分享。

1529
01:04:04.620 --> 01:04:09.660
I want to share about my breast cancer mentor, Clea Shearer.
我想谈谈我的乳腺癌导师，克莉亚·希勒。

1530
01:04:09.960 --> 01:04:16.600
In all of my talking about my journey so far, I haven't had a chance to share about Clea.
在我讲述自己这段经历的过程中，一直没机会提到克莉亚。

1531
01:04:16.880 --> 01:04:18.020
Maybe because.
也许是因为……

1532
01:04:18.340 --> 01:04:18.760
Right.
没错。

1533
01:04:18.760 --> 01:04:20.360
It's so emotional.
这太让人感伤了。

1534
01:04:20.680 --> 01:04:20.940
Right.
确实。

1535
01:04:20.940 --> 01:04:28.960
But after I got my diagnosis, the only person I'd ever known who had had breast cancer was Christina Applegate.
但在我确诊后，我唯一认识的患过乳腺癌的人就是克里斯蒂娜·艾伯盖特。

1536
01:04:29.300 --> 01:04:31.540
We had done a movie together, Hall Pass.
我们曾一起拍过一部电影，叫《放风通行证》。

1537
01:04:31.540 --> 01:04:37.000
It was after she had been through her surgery, and we shared a lot about it on that movie.
那时她刚做完手术，我们在拍戏期间交流了很多关于这方面的事。

1538
01:04:37.320 --> 01:04:38.860
She's such a wonderful person.
她真是个了不起的人。

1539
01:04:38.860 --> 01:04:46.220
And after I got my diagnosis, I texted Christina, and I said, hey, do you have a minute to talk?
在我确诊后，我给克里斯蒂娜发了条短信，问她：“嘿，你有空聊聊吗？”

1540
01:04:46.520 --> 01:04:48.060
And are you somewhere private?
“你现在方便说话吗？”

1541
01:04:48.060 --> 01:04:50.520
I have something personal to share with you.
“我有件私事想跟你说。”

1542
01:04:51.160 --> 01:04:52.940
She texted back, I'm alone.
她回信说：“我一个人，打给我吧。”

1543
01:04:53.100 --> 01:04:53.540
Call me.
打给我。

1544
01:04:54.000 --> 01:04:57.480
And I called her, and I said, I have breast cancer.
我打给她，说：“我得了乳腺癌。”

1545
01:04:58.080 --> 01:05:01.900
And Christina, who is amazing, she goes, I f***ing knew it.
克里斯蒂娜真的很棒，她说：“我他妈就知道。”

1546
01:05:01.980 --> 01:05:03.660
I knew it was breast cancer or Ms.
“我就知道是乳腺癌或者多发性硬化症。”

1547
01:05:03.660 --> 01:05:06.000
I knew that's why you wanted to talk to me in private.
“我知道你为什么想私下跟我谈了。”

1548
01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:08.120
She was like, damn it, damn it.
她当时说：“该死，真该死。”

1549
01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:09.060
Oh, God.
噢，天哪。

1550
01:05:09.320 --> 01:05:12.800
It was just so perfect because I was like, I know, right?
这反应太完美了，我当时就说：“我知道，对吧？”

1551
01:05:13.060 --> 01:05:14.440
She's like, God.
她当时就说，天哪。

1552
01:05:14.960 --> 01:05:16.400
I mean, God, I just love her.
我是说，天哪，我真是太爱她了。

1553
01:05:16.580 --> 01:05:22.660
So she was talking to me, and it was very clear at this point that I was going to have a lot of treatments,
当时她跟我聊天，很明显我已经要接受很多治疗了，

1554
01:05:22.700 --> 01:05:25.180
that chemotherapy was going to be part of my treatment.
化疗将是我治疗的一部分。

1555
01:05:25.640 --> 01:05:29.740
And she said, Jenna, my breast cancer journey was so long ago,
她说，珍娜，我的乳腺癌治疗经历是很久以前的事了，

1556
01:05:29.740 --> 01:05:33.220
and it did not include all the things that you're going to have to go through.
那时候还没有你现在要经历的这些治疗手段。

1557
01:05:33.380 --> 01:05:33.580
Yeah.
是啊。

1558
01:05:33.580 --> 01:05:37.620
But I have a friend who just went through so many of these similar things.
但我有个朋友，她刚经历过很多类似的情况。

1559
01:05:37.740 --> 01:05:38.540
Her name is Clea.
她叫克莉亚。

1560
01:05:38.780 --> 01:05:39.840
Can I put you in touch?
我可以帮你们牵线吗？

1561
01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:42.340
I know she's a stranger to you, but trust me.
我知道对你来说她是陌生人，但相信我。

1562
01:05:42.560 --> 01:05:44.620
So I texted Clea.
于是我给克莉亚发了短信。

1563
01:05:44.940 --> 01:05:46.580
Just someone you'd never met?
就是一个你从未见过的人？

1564
01:05:46.880 --> 01:05:47.740
Never met.
从未见过。

1565
01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:48.140
Right.
没错。

1566
01:05:48.460 --> 01:05:55.380
She sprang into action in a way that I don't even know how to ever thank her for.
她立刻行动起来，我甚至不知道该怎么感谢她才好。

1567
01:05:56.520 --> 01:05:58.500
She answered every question.
她回答了我每一个问题。

1568
01:05:59.560 --> 01:06:02.660
She comforted all of my nerves.
她安抚了我所有的焦虑。

1569
01:06:03.140 --> 01:06:06.060
She offered suggestions and guidance.
她提供了建议和指导。

1570
01:06:06.390 --> 01:06:11.660
But most of all, she was just a person who had done it before and was okay.
但最重要的是，她是一个过来人，而且她现在很好。

1571
01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:16.180
And I needed to hear from that person so much.
我太需要听到这样的人说话了。

1572
01:06:16.320 --> 01:06:16.500
Yeah.
是啊。

1573
01:06:16.500 --> 01:06:21.660
And she has become an absolute dear friend of mine through all of this.
通过这一切，她成了我非常亲密的朋友。

1574
01:06:21.880 --> 01:06:23.320
We are still friends.
我们现在还是朋友。

1575
01:06:23.660 --> 01:06:25.260
She still counsels me.
她依然在开导我。

1576
01:06:25.660 --> 01:06:30.000
And she has inspired me to take on that role for others.
她还激励我为其他人扮演同样的角色。

1577
01:06:30.180 --> 01:06:30.780
And so—
所以——

1578
01:06:31.160 --> 01:06:31.980
And you are.
而你也确实做到了。

1579
01:06:32.160 --> 01:06:33.260
And I have been.
我一直在做。

1580
01:06:33.380 --> 01:06:33.580
Yeah.
是啊。

1581
01:06:33.580 --> 01:06:40.320
And what it taught me was that going through this whole thing, this whole mess of breast
